# Read by every zsh, interactive or not, and before .zshrc.
#
# .zshrc sets the same path further down, which covers interactive shells; this
# is what puts ~/.local/bin in front for non-interactive ones — scripts with a
# `#!/usr/bin/env zsh` line, `zsh -c`, and anything a tool spawns. uv installs
# there (ruff, basedpyright, debugpy, djlint via bootstrap.sh).
#
# The three directories are the same three .zshrc lists, in the same order. ~/bin
# used to be missing from this copy, so the scripts in it — dev and new-session —
# resolved only in an interactive shell. Everything still worked, because tmux is
# started from one and hands its environment to the popup, but the moment that
# chain does not begin interactively the picker cannot find new-session.
#
# $HOME rather than a literal path: uv's installer wrote this file with
# /Users/gabrielgellner hardcoded, which would have expanded to nothing useful
# on the Linux machine had it ever been synced.
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$HOME/.cargo/bin:$HOME/bin:$PATH"

# Keep Claude Code out of the alternate screen, so its output lands in Neovim's
# terminal scrollback.
#
# Claude is a full-screen float in nvim (plugins/claudecode.lua), and <C-x>
# leaves terminal mode to scroll and yank its output. That stops working the
# moment Claude renders in the alternate screen: alt-screen content never
# scrolls off, so 'scrollback' has nothing to record and <C-u> has nowhere to
# go. Measured in the live float, forcing 200 lines through it — the buffer
# held 44 lines against a 44-line window, and 217 with this set. <C-x><C-u>
# scrolls, and keeps scrolling, only in the second case.
#
# The mouse wheel keeps working either way, which is what makes the fault
# confusing rather than obvious: Claude requests mouse tracking (?1000h ?1002h
# ?1003h ?1006h), so nvim forwards the wheel to Claude and Claude scrolls its
# own history. The content is genuinely there; the keyboard is just asking a
# different buffer for it. PageUp/PageDown in *terminal* mode reach Claude's
# scrollback and are the fallback if this variable ever stops being honoured —
# <C-b>, <C-u>, <M-Up> and <S-Up> were all measured doing nothing there.
#
# Set here rather than left to chance because the two machines disagreed on the
# same 2.1.236 with the same config: the laptop's float held 3996 lines while
# the Linux one held 44. Neither sets any CLAUDE_CODE_* variable, so the
# difference is outside these dotfiles — a rollout flag or a terminal-capability
# check, not something the repo controls. The binary carries
# CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_VIRTUAL_SCROLL alongside this, which suggests alt-screen
# arrived with a newer scrolling feature. Pinning it makes both machines
# explicit rather than accidental.
#
# Verified as the real switch, not a coincidence: =1 emits no ESC[?1049h, =0
# emits it.
export CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_ALTERNATE_SCREEN=1
